<section class="section reveal" id="what-are-variables">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>What Are Variables?</h2>

  <p>Before you can build AI models, automate tasks, or process data, you need to understand how Python stores and retrieves information. That is what variables do — they give a name to a piece of data so you can use it later. Think of a variable as a labelled storage box: the label (the variable name) tells you what is inside, and the box (the memory location) holds the actual data. The critical insight is that in Python, variables are not boxes at all — they are labels that point to objects in memory.</p>

  <p>Unlike languages that require you to declare types upfront, Python uses dynamic typing. This means Python figures out the type of your variable automatically based on the value you assign. This makes Python exceptionally friendly for beginners and incredibly productive for data science, where you frequently switch between numbers, text, and complex data structures within the same script or notebook.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Imagine a vast warehouse full of boxes. Each box contains a specific piece of data. The labels (variable names) do not belong to the boxes — they belong to you. You can stick your label on any box you want, move it to a different box, or remove it entirely. In Python, when you write <code>x = 5</code>, you are sticking the label 'x' onto the box containing the number 5. Later, when you write <code>x = "hello"</code>, you remove the 'x' label from the number box and stick it onto a string box. The number 5 remains in the warehouse — your label just moved.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">How Python Variable Assignment Works</div>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 800 220" style="width:100%;max-width:800px;margin:0 auto;display:block">
      <defs>
        <marker id="arr" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="6" refX="8" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,3 L0,6" fill="#1A3A6B"/></marker>
      </defs>
      <rect x="20" y="30" width="160" height="70" rx="10" fill="#f1f5f9" stroke="#1A3A6B" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="100" y="58" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1A3A6B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">STEP 1</text>
      <text x="100" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1e293b" font-size="11">age = 25</text>
      <line x1="180" y1="65" x2="230" y2="65" stroke="#1A3A6B" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
      <rect x="240" y="30" width="160" height="70" rx="10" fill="#f1f5f9" stroke="#A4421F" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="320" y="58" text-anchor="middle" fill="#A4421F" font-size="10" font-weight="600">STEP 2</text>
      <text x="320" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1e293b" font-size="11">Python creates int(25)</text>
      <line x1="400" y1="65" x2="450" y2="65" stroke="#1A3A6B" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
      <rect x="460" y="30" width="160" height="70" rx="10" fill="#f1f5f9" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="540" y="58" text-anchor="middle" fill="#16a34a" font-size="10" font-weight="600">STEP 3</text>
      <text x="540" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1e293b" font-size="11">Name 'age' → object</text>
      <line x1="620" y1="65" x2="670" y2="65" stroke="#1A3A6B" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
      <rect x="680" y="25" width="100" height="80" rx="10" fill="rgba(54,207,201,0.1)" stroke="#1A3A6B" stroke-width="2"/>
      <text x="730" y="55" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1A3A6B" font-size="10" font-weight="700">MEMORY</text>
      <text x="730" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1e293b" font-size="18" font-weight="700">25</text>
      <rect x="20" y="130" width="760" height="70" rx="8" fill="rgba(54,207,201,0.04)" stroke="rgba(54,207,201,0.1)"/>
      <text x="40" y="155" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">1. You write the assignment statement</text>
      <text x="40" y="175" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">2. Python creates the integer object 25 in memory</text>
      <text x="400" y="155" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">3. The name 'age' becomes a reference to that object</text>
      <text x="400" y="175" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">4. You can now use 'age' anywhere to access value 25</text>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <p>When you write <code>age = 25</code>, three things happen behind the scenes. First, Python reads your assignment statement. Second, it creates an integer object containing the value 25 and stores it somewhere in memory. Third, it creates a reference — a pointer — from the name <code>age</code> to that memory location. From that point on, whenever you use <code>age</code> in your code, Python follows that reference to retrieve the value 25. This is why you can reassign variables: the name simply points to a different object.</p>

  <p>Here is a simple example showing variable creation and reassignment:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Creating variables — no type declaration needed</span>
<span class="va">name</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"SustainSys"</span>       <span class="cm"># str: text data</span>
<span class="va">version</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">3.2</span>              <span class="cm"># float: decimal number</span>
<span class="va">is_active</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">True</span>           <span class="cm"># bool: True or False</span>
<span class="va">user_count</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">1500</span>          <span class="cm"># int: whole number</span>

<span class="cm"># Reassignment — the type can change</span>
<span class="va">version</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"3.2.1"</span>          <span class="cm"># now it's a string!</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">type</span>(<span class="va">version</span>))       <span class="cm"># &lt;class 'str'&gt;</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We create four variables with different types. Python infers each type automatically based on the literal syntax. When we reassign <code>version</code> from a float to a string, Python does not complain — it simply creates a new string object and points the name to it. The old float object is eventually cleaned up by Python's garbage collector, which reclaims memory from unused objects.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Variables are names that reference objects in memory — not containers holding data</li>
      <li>Python uses dynamic typing: the type is determined by the value, not declared upfront</li>
      <li>Reassignment creates a new reference — the old object may be garbage collected</li>
      <li>Use <code>type()</code> to inspect what type a variable currently holds</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="1">
      <p>What happens when you write <code>x = 10</code> then <code>x = "hello"</code>?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) Python throws a TypeError because x was already an integer</li>
        <li>B) x now references a string object; the integer 10 may be garbage collected</li>
        <li>C) Both values are stored and x becomes a list</li>
        <li>D) The second assignment is silently ignored</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">Python uses dynamic typing, so x simply becomes a reference to the new string object "hello". The integer 10 is no longer referenced by any name and may be cleaned up by the garbage collector.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

