<section class="section reveal" id="core-data-types">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Python's Five Core Data Types</h2>

  <p>Every value in Python belongs to a type, and understanding types is critical for all AI work. When you load a CSV dataset, each column has a type. When you feed data to a machine learning model, it expects specific numeric types. When you build an API endpoint, you need to serialize types correctly. Let us explore the five fundamental types you will encounter most often in any Python program.</p>

  <p>These types form the foundation of all more complex data structures (lists, dictionaries, etc.) that we will study in future lessons. Mastering these five types now will make everything else fall into place.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Python Core Data Types at a Glance</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>int — Integers</h5>
        <p>Whole numbers: positive, negative, or zero. Used for counts, indices, array dimensions, and IDs. Python integers have arbitrary precision — they can be as large as your memory allows, unlike C or Java.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>42</code>, <code>-7</code>, <code>1_000_000</code></li>
          <li>AI: batch sizes, epoch counts, pixel values</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>float — Decimals</h5>
        <p>Numbers with decimal points. Stored as 64-bit IEEE 754 double precision. Note: tiny rounding errors are inevitable and normal due to binary representation.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>3.14</code>, <code>-0.001</code>, <code>1e-5</code></li>
          <li>AI: model weights, loss values, learning rates</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>str — Strings</h5>
        <p>Sequences of Unicode characters. Immutable — you cannot change individual characters in place. Strings form the basis of all text processing and NLP work.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>"hello"</code>, <code>'single quotes'</code>, <code>"""multi\nline"""</code></li>
          <li>AI: text processing, NLP, prompt engineering</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>bool — Booleans</h5>
        <p><code>True</code> or <code>False</code>. Actually a subclass of int where True=1 and False=0. Used in conditions, filtering, and control flow.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>True</code>, <code>False</code>, <code>3 > 2</code> (evaluates to True)</li>
          <li>AI: feature flags, masking, binary labels</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Integers in Machine Learning</h3>
  <p>Integers are the backbone of counting and indexing. In AI, you use integers constantly: specifying the number of training epochs, batch size for mini-batch training, the number of neurons in a layer, or the index of a sample in your dataset. Integer arithmetic with the floor division operator <code>//</code> is also crucial for calculating how many batches fit in a dataset or how to split data into folds.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Integer examples in AI contexts</span>
<span class="va">batch_size</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">32</span>
<span class="va">epochs</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">100</span>
<span class="va">hidden_neurons</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">256</span>

<span class="cm"># Underscore separators for readability</span>
<span class="va">dataset_size</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">1_000_000</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">dataset_size</span>)  <span class="cm"># 1000000</span>

<span class="cm"># Integer arithmetic</span>
<span class="va">total_batches</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">dataset_size</span> <span class="op">//</span> <span class="va">batch_size</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Training: </span>{<span class="va">total_batches</span>}<span class="st"> batches per epoch"</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We define variables commonly used in machine learning training loops. The <code>//</code> operator performs floor division, which rounds down to the nearest integer. This gives us exactly how many complete batches fit in our dataset. The f-string syntax (formatted string literal) lets us embed the calculation result directly in the output message.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Floats, Strings, and Booleans</h3>
  <p>Floats represent decimal numbers and are essential for any numerical computation — neural network weights, loss values, learning rates, and probability outputs. A critical thing to remember is that floats have tiny precision errors due to how computers store decimal numbers in binary. Never use <code>==</code> to compare two floats directly; instead, use <code>abs(a - b) &lt; epsilon</code> or NumPy's <code>isclose()</code> function.</p>

  <p>Strings are sequences of Unicode text. They are immutable, meaning you cannot change individual characters in place. This might seem like a limitation, but it makes strings safe to use in dictionaries and as function arguments. Booleans result from comparisons (<code>3 > 2</code>) and conditions (<code>name == "Alice"</code>) and are heavily used in filtering and control flow.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Float precision — watch out!</span>
<span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0.1</span> <span class="op">+</span> <span class="nm">0.2</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">==</span> <span class="nm">0.3</span>)  <span class="cm"># False! Not exact due to binary representation</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">x</span>)             <span class="cm"># 0.30000000000000004</span>

<span class="cm"># String examples and methods</span>
<span class="va">text</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"machine learning"</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">text</span>.<span class="fn">upper</span>())        <span class="cm"># MACHINE LEARNING</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">text</span>.<span class="fn">split</span>())       <span class="cm"># ['machine', 'learning']</span>

<span class="cm"># Boolean examples from comparisons</span>
<span class="va">is_trained</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">95</span> <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">90</span>  <span class="cm"># True</span>
<span class="va">model_ready</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">True</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">model_ready</span> <span class="op">and</span> <span class="va">is_trained</span>)  <span class="cm"># True</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We demonstrate the classic float precision issue: 0.1 + 0.2 does not exactly equal 0.3 in binary floating-point. We show string methods like <code>upper()</code> and <code>split()</code> which are essential for text processing and NLP pipelines. Finally, we create booleans from comparisons and combine them with logical operators <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, and <code>not</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Financial AI:</strong> Using the wrong data type causes financial disasters. Integer overflow when calculating portfolio values, float rounding errors in price calculations, or type mismatches in currency conversions lead to significant losses. Always validate and cast types carefully in fintech systems.</p>
    <p><strong>In Life Sciences AI:</strong> Medical datasets mix integers (patient IDs, cell counts) with floats (measurements, concentrations) and strings (names, diagnoses). Type confusion silently corrupts analysis before you notice the error.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><code>int</code>: whole numbers, arbitrary precision, used for counts and indices</li>
      <li><code>float</code>: decimal numbers with IEEE 754 precision; never use == to compare</li>
      <li><code>str</code>: immutable sequences of Unicode; powerful string methods available</li>
      <li><code>bool</code>: True or False, result of comparisons, subclass of int</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="2">
      <p>Why should you avoid comparing floats with ==?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) Python doesn't support the == operator for floats</li>
        <li>B) Floats have tiny precision errors due to binary representation; use abs(a-b) < epsilon instead</li>
        <li>C) It's slower than comparing integers</li>
        <li>D) Floats automatically round to the nearest integer</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">Floats are stored in binary format (IEEE 754), which cannot exactly represent all decimal numbers. Operations like 0.1 + 0.2 produce results very close to but not exactly 0.3. Always compare floats with a small epsilon tolerance.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

