<section class="section reveal" id="type-conversion">
  <span class="section-label">Practical Skills</span>
  <h2>Type Conversion and Type Checking</h2>

  <p>In real-world AI projects, data rarely arrives in the perfect format. You might read a CSV file where every value is a string, even the numbers. You might receive API responses where numeric values are encoded as text. Type conversion — also called type casting — is how you transform data from one type to another.</p>

  <p>Python supports two kinds of conversion: implicit (automatic) and explicit (you tell Python what to do). Understanding both is essential for writing bug-free data pipelines and avoiding silent data corruption.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Type Conversion Decision Flowchart</div>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 800 320" style="width:100%;max-width:800px;margin:0 auto;display:block">
      <defs>
        <marker id="arr2" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="6" refX="8" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,3 L0,6" fill="#1A3A6B"/></marker>
      </defs>
      <rect x="300" y="10" width="200" height="45" rx="22" fill="rgba(54,207,201,0.15)" stroke="#1A3A6B" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="400" y="38" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1A3A6B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">Need to change type?</text>
      <line x1="350" y1="55" x2="180" y2="100" stroke="#1A3A6B" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr2)"/>
      <text x="240" y="75" fill="#16a34a" font-size="9">Automatic</text>
      <rect x="60" y="100" width="240" height="55" rx="8" fill="#f1f5f9" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="180" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="#16a34a" font-size="10" font-weight="600">IMPLICIT CONVERSION</text>
      <text x="180" y="140" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">int + float → float (auto)</text>
      <line x1="450" y1="55" x2="620" y2="100" stroke="#1A3A6B" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr2)"/>
      <text x="550" y="75" fill="#C0522F" font-size="9">Manual</text>
      <rect x="500" y="100" width="240" height="55" rx="8" fill="#f1f5f9" stroke="#C0522F" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="620" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C0522F" font-size="10" font-weight="600">EXPLICIT CONVERSION</text>
      <text x="620" y="140" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">int(), float(), str(), bool()</text>
      <rect x="60" y="180" width="240" height="120" rx="8" fill="rgba(6,255,165,0.04)" stroke="rgba(6,255,165,0.15)"/>
      <text x="80" y="205" fill="#16a34a" font-size="9" font-weight="600">SAFE — Python handles it:</text>
      <text x="80" y="225" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">5 + 2.0 → 7.0</text>
      <text x="80" y="245" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">True + 1 → 2</text>
      <text x="80" y="265" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">False * 10 → 0</text>
      <text x="80" y="285" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="9">No data loss risk</text>
      <rect x="500" y="180" width="240" height="120" rx="8" fill="rgba(255,149,0,0.04)" stroke="rgba(255,149,0,0.15)"/>
      <text x="520" y="205" fill="#C0522F" font-size="9" font-weight="600">CAUTION — You decide:</text>
      <text x="520" y="225" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">int(3.9) → 3 (truncated!)</text>
      <text x="520" y="245" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">float("42") → 42.0</text>
      <text x="520" y="265" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">int("hello") → ERROR</text>
      <text x="520" y="285" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="9">Possible data loss or errors</text>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Explicit type conversion examples</span>
<span class="va">price_str</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"49.99"</span>
<span class="va">price</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">float</span>(<span class="va">price_str</span>)     <span class="cm"># str → float</span>
<span class="va">quantity</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">int</span>(<span class="st">"3"</span>)           <span class="cm"># str → int</span>
<span class="va">total</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">price</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="va">quantity</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Total: $</span>{<span class="va">total</span>:<span class="st">.2f</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)  <span class="cm"># Total: $149.97</span>

<span class="cm"># Dangerous: int() truncates, doesn't round</span>
<span class="va">score</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">98.7</span>
<span class="va">truncated</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">int</span>(<span class="va">score</span>)         <span class="cm"># 98 — data loss!</span>
<span class="va">rounded</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">round</span>(<span class="va">score</span>)      <span class="cm"># 99 — correct</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We convert a price from string (as it might arrive from CSV or API) to float for calculation. Note that <code>int()</code> truncates decimals — it chops off everything after the decimal point rather than rounding. If you want proper rounding, use <code>round()</code> instead. The f-string format <code>{value:.2f}</code> displays a float with exactly 2 decimal places.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Type Checking Functions</h3>
  <p>Beyond conversion, you often need to check what type a variable currently is. The <code>type()</code> function returns the exact type, while <code>isinstance()</code> checks if a variable is an instance of a specific type (including subclasses). For robust AI code, always validate types before using them in computations.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Type checking with type() and isinstance()</span>
<span class="va">value</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">42</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">type</span>(<span class="va">value</span>))            <span class="cm"># &lt;class 'int'&gt;</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">isinstance</span>(<span class="va">value</span>, <span class="dt">int</span>))  <span class="cm"># True</span>

<span class="cm"># Boolean is subclass of int — watch out!</span>
<span class="va">flag</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">True</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">isinstance</span>(<span class="va">flag</span>, <span class="dt">bool</span>))  <span class="cm"># True</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">isinstance</span>(<span class="va">flag</span>, <span class="dt">int</span>))   <span class="cm"># Also True!</span>

<span class="cm"># Validate before using — safe conversion pattern</span>
<span class="va">user_input</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"abc"</span>
<span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(<span class="va">user_input</span>, <span class="dt">str</span>):
    <span class="kw">try</span>:
        <span class="va">num</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">int</span>(<span class="va">user_input</span>)
    <span class="kw">except</span> <span class="bi">ValueError</span>:
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Could not convert to int"</span>)</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We use <code>type()</code> to get the exact type and <code>isinstance()</code> to check membership (including subclasses). Note that <code>bool</code> is a subclass of <code>int</code>, so <code>isinstance(True, int)</code> returns True. We show a try-except pattern to safely attempt conversion and handle errors gracefully — an essential technique in production AI code that processes untrusted data.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Implicit conversion happens automatically (e.g., int + float → float)</li>
      <li>Explicit conversion requires calling <code>int()</code>, <code>float()</code>, <code>str()</code>, or <code>bool()</code></li>
      <li><code>int()</code> truncates decimals — use <code>round()</code> for proper rounding</li>
      <li>Use <code>isinstance()</code> to safely check types; use try-except for risky conversions</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="1">
      <p>What is the output of <code>int(9.8)</code>?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) 10 (rounded up)</li>
        <li>B) 9 (truncated)</li>
        <li>C) 9.8 (no conversion)</li>
        <li>D) Error: cannot convert float to int</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer"><code>int()</code> truncates the decimal portion, so <code>int(9.8)</code> returns 9. It does not round; it simply drops everything after the decimal point. Use <code>round(9.8)</code> to get 10.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

