<section class="section reveal" id="naming-conventions">
  <span class="section-label">Best Practices</span>
  <h2>Naming Conventions & Production Code Style</h2>

  <p>Writing code that works is step one. Writing code that other people (including future you six months from now) can understand is step two — and it is equally important. In AI teams, you will work with data scientists, ML engineers, and software developers. Following Python naming conventions makes your code instantly readable to anyone on the team and is considered a mark of professionalism.</p>

  <p>The Python community follows PEP 8, the official style guide. The core idea is that clear, descriptive names make code self-documenting. When someone reads <code>learning_rate = 0.001</code>, they immediately understand the purpose. When someone reads <code>lr = 0.001</code>, they have to think about it or look for context.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Python Naming Convention Reference</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Good Variable Names</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><code>learning_rate = 0.001</code></li>
          <li><code>batch_size = 32</code></li>
          <li><code>model_accuracy = 0.95</code></li>
          <li><code>is_trained = True</code></li>
          <li><code>num_epochs = 100</code></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Bad Variable Names</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><code>lr = 0.001</code> (too cryptic)</li>
          <li><code>x = 32</code> (meaningless)</li>
          <li><code>ModelAccuracy = 0.95</code> (PascalCase)</li>
          <li><code>FLAG = True</code> (vague)</li>
          <li><code>a1 = 100</code> (unclear purpose)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Naming Rules (PEP 8)</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Variables: <code>snake_case</code></li>
          <li>Functions: <code>snake_case</code></li>
          <li>Classes: <code>PascalCase</code></li>
          <li>Constants: <code>UPPER_SNAKE</code></li>
          <li>Private: prefix with <code>_</code></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>Boolean variables should start with <code>is_</code>, <code>has_</code>, or <code>can_</code> to make it obvious they are truth values. Numeric variables should include descriptive context. Constants (values that do not change throughout execution) use <code>UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</code>. Private variables — those meant for internal use within a module — start with a single underscore to signal they are not part of the public API.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Good naming in an AI model configuration</span>
<span class="va">MAX_ITERATIONS</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">1000</span>  <span class="cm"># constant</span>
<span class="va">learning_rate</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0.001</span>    <span class="cm"># variable</span>
<span class="va">is_pretrained</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">True</span>     <span class="cm"># boolean</span>
<span class="va">has_dropout</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">True</span>       <span class="cm"># boolean flag</span>
<span class="va">_internal_seed</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">42</span>        <span class="cm"># private variable</span>

<span class="cm"># Clear function names</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">calculate_accuracy</span>(<span class="va">predictions</span>, <span class="va">labels</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Compute accuracy between predictions and labels."""</span>
    <span class="va">matches</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">predictions</span> <span class="op">==</span> <span class="va">labels</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="va">matches</span>) <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">labels</span>)</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We demonstrate proper naming across constants, variables, booleans, and private values. Each name is self-documenting and follows PEP 8 conventions. The function <code>calculate_accuracy</code> is a verb phrase that clearly describes what it does. The docstring (triple-quoted string) explains the purpose and is considered best practice for all non-trivial functions.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Production Teams:</strong> Code reviews and maintenance become painful when variable names are cryptic. Following conventions makes onboarding faster, reduces bugs from misunderstanding, and shows you understand professional standards.</p>
    <p><strong>In Long-Term Projects:</strong> Six months from now, you will look at your own code and struggle to understand <code>x = 0.001</code> versus <code>learning_rate = 0.001</code>. Clear names are an investment in your future self and team productivity.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Use <code>snake_case</code> for variables and functions</li>
      <li>Use <code>PascalCase</code> for class names only</li>
      <li>Use <code>UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</code> for constants</li>
      <li>Prefix booleans with <code>is_</code>, <code>has_</code>, or <code>can_</code></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="0">
      <p>Which variable name follows Python conventions?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) <code>model_dropout_rate = 0.2</code></li>
        <li>B) <code>modelDropoutRate = 0.2</code></li>
        <li>C) <code>Model_Dropout_Rate = 0.2</code></li>
        <li>D) <code>MDR = 0.2</code></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">PEP 8 specifies <code>snake_case</code> for variables: <code>model_dropout_rate</code>. Option B is camelCase (JavaScript style), C is PascalCase (classes only), and D is too cryptic.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

