<section class="section reveal" id="special-values">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Concepts</span>
  <h2>None and Special Values</h2>

  <p>Python has one more fundamental type you need to know: <code>NoneType</code>, whose only value is <code>None</code>. <code>None</code> represents the absence of a value — it is not False, not zero, not an empty string. It is explicitly nothing. You will encounter <code>None</code> constantly in AI code: when functions do not return a value, when dictionary lookups fail with <code>.get()</code>, when optional parameters are not provided, or when data is missing.</p>

  <p>Understanding the difference between <code>None</code>, <code>False</code>, <code>0</code>, and empty strings is crucial for debugging. These are all "falsy" in boolean contexts, but they are not equal to each other, and treating them as equivalent is a common source of bugs.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># None vs other falsy values</span>
<span class="va">nothing</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">None</span>
<span class="va">false_value</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">False</span>
<span class="va">zero</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0</span>
<span class="va">empty_string</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">""</span>

<span class="cm"># All are falsy in if statements</span>
<span class="kw">if</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="va">nothing</span>: <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"None is falsy"</span>)
<span class="kw">if</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="va">zero</span>: <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"0 is falsy"</span>)

<span class="cm"># But they are NOT equal</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">nothing</span> <span class="op">==</span> <span class="kw">False</span>)  <span class="cm"># False</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">nothing</span> <span class="op">is</span> <span class="kw">None</span>)   <span class="cm"># True — always use 'is'</span>

<span class="cm"># Correct way to check for None</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process_data</span>(<span class="va">value</span>):
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">value</span> <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="kw">None</span>:
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"No data provided"</span>)
    <span class="kw">else</span>:
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Processing: </span>{<span class="va">value</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We demonstrate that <code>None</code>, <code>False</code>, 0, and empty strings are all falsy — they evaluate to False in boolean contexts. However, they are not equal to each other. The critical lesson is to use <code>is None</code> and <code>is not None</code> for checking if a value is None — never use <code>== None</code>. The <code>is</code> operator checks identity (same object in memory), while <code>==</code> checks equality (same value), and they behave differently for None.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><code>None</code> represents the absence of a value — it is a distinct type</li>
      <li><code>None</code>, <code>False</code>, 0, and <code>""</code> are all falsy but not equal</li>
      <li>Always use <code>is None</code> and <code>is not None</code> to check for None</li>
      <li>Never use <code>== None</code> — it is incorrect Python style</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

